-› API Documentation
-By default, Pydantic will attempt to coerce values to the desired type when possible. For
-example, you can pass the string '123' as the input forthe int number type, and it will be
-converted to the value 123 . This coercion behavior is useful in many scenarios - think: UIDs,
-URL parameters, HTTP headers, environment variables, dates, etc.
-However, there are also situations where this is not desirable, and you want Pydantic to error
-instead of coercing data.
-To better support this use case, Pydantic provides a "strict mode". When strict mode is
-enabled, Pydantic will be much less lenient when coercing data, and will instead error if the
-data is not of the correct type.
-Most of the time, strict mode will only allow instances of the type to be provided, although
-looser rules may apply to JSON input (for instance, the date and time types allow strings even
-in strict mode).
-The strict behavior for each type can be found in the standard library types documentation,
-and is summarized in the conversion table.
